        <!-- Module 1: Cinematic Scenes in Blender -->
        <section id="module1" class="module-1">
            <h2 class="module-title">1. Creating Cinematic Scenes in Blender</h2>
            <p>This module guides you through building a visually stunning cinematic sequence in Blender. We'll use kitbashed assets, simple textures, volumetric lighting and structured planning to create a sequence reminiscent of a film intro.</p>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li><strong>Gather references and storyboard:</strong> Collect concept art or movie stills that match the mood (e.g., Star Wars space battle). Sketch a simple storyboard for the sequence to clarify camera angles and timing.</li>
                <li><strong>Kitbash your scene:</strong> Use a kitbash library or asset packs to quickly assemble starships, space stations, terrain and props. The 80lv interview with Karl&nbsp;Poyzer explains that kitbashing accelerates scene building and allows you to focus on art direction rather than modelling every object【416691231949832†L88-L143】.</li>
                <li><strong>Apply simple textures:</strong> Keep textures lightweight but convincing. Use cube‑projected image textures or triplanar maps on your models for quick yet consistent results【416691231949832†L88-L143】. You can add subtle wear using procedural textures like JSplacement patterns (see <em>resources</em>).</li>
                <li><strong>Animate and block out timing:</strong> In Blender’s Dope Sheet or Non‑Linear Animation (NLA) editor, block out the key frames for your ship movements and camera. Use simple shapes as placeholders if final assets aren’t ready.</li>
                <li><strong>Lighting and atmosphere:</strong> To achieve cinematic realism, use an HDRI environment for global illumination and add a volumetric cube with a warm orange light to create depth and mood【416691231949832†L88-L143】. Adjust the density to simulate space fog or dust.</li>
                <li><strong>Render by layers:</strong> When working on limited hardware, render different elements (ships, environment, effects) as separate view layers. This approach lets you composite complex shots in the compositor without overloading your GPU【416691231949832†L126-L130】.</li>
                <li><strong>Export final camera and animation:</strong> Ensure your camera animation is polished before exporting. Name the camera clearly and push NLA strips down for each action (if using NLA) to prepare for glTF export (see Module 2).</li>
            </ol>
