        <!-- Module 3: Environment & Lighting in Godot -->
        <section id="module3" class="module-3">
            <h2 class="module-title">3. Setting Up Environment & Lighting</h2>
            <p>After importing your scene, set up the environment and lighting to achieve realistic results in Godot. Good lighting makes a huge difference in the final look of your cinematic.</p>
            <ol class="steps">
                <li><strong>Add a WorldEnvironment node:</strong> In your Godot scene, add a <em>WorldEnvironment</em>. Under <em>Environment</em> properties, switch the <em>Background</em> mode to <em>Sky</em> and assign a <em>Sky</em> resource.</li>
                <li><strong>Use a physical sky or HDRI:</strong> For realistic outdoor scenes, choose the <em>PhysicalSkyMaterial</em>. Set the ground color and sky energy (values above 1.0 brightens the scene)【305744022770720†L87-L117】. For space scenes, load an HDRI into a PanoramaSky resource.</li>
                <li><strong>Add a directional light:</strong> Add a <em>DirectionalLight3D</em> node to simulate the sun. Adjust its rotation to match the angle of your scene and modify <em>Energy</em> and <em>Specular</em> to achieve the look you want【305744022770720†L87-L117】.</li>
                <li><strong>Ambient and indirect lighting:</strong> Under the WorldEnvironment, enable <em>Ambient Light</em>. Increase the <em>Energy</em> and adjust color tint to soften shadows. Consider enabling <em>Screen‑Space Reflection (SSR)</em> and <em>Glow</em> for additional realism.</li>
                <li><strong>Optional volumetrics:</strong> For atmospheric effects, enable <em>Volumetric Fog</em>. You can also add volumetric clouds via custom shaders or Godot’s built‑in <em>ProceduralSkyMaterial</em> with volumetric modules【305744022770720†L87-L117】.</li>
            </ol>
